WEBVTT

00:00.080 --> 00:01.240
So what are the nulls?

00:01.280 --> 00:06.720
Imagine you are filling out a form and there will be usually like fields that are required and another

00:06.720 --> 00:08.080
fields that are optional.

00:08.080 --> 00:09.320
So what usually happens?

00:09.320 --> 00:15.160
We leave those optional fields unanswered so we don't provide any values and we leave it empty.

00:15.200 --> 00:20.840
And now once we are done filling out the form and we click on register, the data will be inserted into

00:20.840 --> 00:22.000
database tables.

00:22.000 --> 00:22.960
So now what can happen?

00:22.960 --> 00:28.760
The fields where you have provided answers and values can be filled inside the table, while the unanswered

00:28.760 --> 00:31.200
fields will have no value.

00:31.200 --> 00:33.640
And this is what we call in SQL a null.

00:33.640 --> 00:37.320
So in databases a null means nothing unknown.

00:37.360 --> 00:39.160
It is not equal to anything.

00:39.160 --> 00:43.480
So it is not equal to zero or empty string or blank space.

00:43.520 --> 00:45.360
And null is simply nothing.

00:45.640 --> 00:48.720
It tells us there is no value and it is missing.

00:48.720 --> 00:51.640
It's like saying I do not know what this value is.

00:51.640 --> 00:54.080
So this is what a null means in SQL.

00:58.080 --> 00:58.800
All right friends.

00:58.800 --> 01:05.200
So now we're going to do a deep dive into spatial SQL functions on how to handle the nulls inside our

01:05.200 --> 01:05.640
data.

01:05.680 --> 01:08.640
Now in some scenarios we have nulls inside our tables.

01:08.840 --> 01:14.800
And we would like to go and remove it and replace it with a new value like for example 40.

01:14.840 --> 01:17.440
And in order to do that in SQL, we have two functions.

01:17.440 --> 01:19.390
The first one called is a null.

01:19.630 --> 01:22.430
And the second one called coalesce.

01:22.470 --> 01:27.150
But now let's say that we have another scenario where we have a value inside our table like the 40,

01:27.190 --> 01:29.630
and we want to go and make it as a null.

01:29.670 --> 01:31.590
So now we are doing the exact opposite.

01:31.590 --> 01:34.310
We are replacing the value with a null.

01:34.310 --> 01:36.830
And for that we have the SQL function null.

01:36.870 --> 01:37.190
If.

01:37.270 --> 01:40.430
So as you can see with those two scenarios we are replacing stuff.

01:40.590 --> 01:43.830
So from null to value or from value to null.

01:43.830 --> 01:48.190
So they are really helpful in order to manipulate the data inside our databases.

01:48.230 --> 01:52.030
Now moving on to another scenario where we don't want to manipulate anything.

01:52.030 --> 01:53.390
We want just to check.

01:53.390 --> 01:55.830
So we don't want to replace or convert anything.

01:55.830 --> 01:59.830
We want just to check in our database whether we have a null value.

01:59.830 --> 02:02.430
And for that we have a function called isnull.

02:02.470 --> 02:04.910
But between the is and null there is like space.

02:04.910 --> 02:06.830
It is different than the first function.

02:06.830 --> 02:10.670
So if you apply is null you're going to get a boolean true or false.

02:10.670 --> 02:12.470
For this scenario you will get true.

02:12.510 --> 02:15.990
Or the second option you can go and check whether the value is not null.

02:15.990 --> 02:17.750
So we can use is not null.

02:17.950 --> 02:19.990
And for this example you're going to get false.

02:19.990 --> 02:23.030
So in the output we are getting a boolean true or false.

02:23.030 --> 02:24.870
So those keywords are really amazing.

02:24.870 --> 02:28.270
In order to check whether we have nulls inside our data.

02:28.270 --> 02:33.270
So this is the big picture of all functions that we have in SQL in order to handle the nulls.

02:33.270 --> 02:36.230
So now let's go and understand those functions one by one.
